WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.530
Whether you are hand-manipulating
and reforming stitches or knitting 1x1

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.340
ribbing with the ribber,
plan ahead for perfectly matched seams.

3
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:16.600
Having an uninterrupted seam is the goal.

4
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:25.040
As you're setting up your knitting,
each edge will be a bit different.

5
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.580
When knitting with a ribber,
make sure that one edge,

6
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:38.580
(let's say the left side) has two needles
on the ribber and the opposite side

7
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.780
of the knitted piece will
have one needle on the ribber.

8
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.820
This will form purl stitches on the edges

9
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.660
with a 1x1 needle
arrangement in between.

10
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.500
For your second piece,
place two needles on the right side

11
00:00:54.530 --> 00:01:02.060
on the ribber and one needle on the
opposite or the left side on the ribber.

12
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:04.180
This is what the two pieces would look

13
00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:09.850
like next to each other, as if you were
looking at them from the private side.

14
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:17.080
You'll see two purl stitches on one
edge and one purl stitch on the other.

15
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.820
When seaming, use a full stitch mattress
stitch and the 1x1 sequence is

16
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.620
uninterrupted.
Now when you're hand-manipulating the same

17
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:35.570
holds true if you're reforming
for 1x1 ribbing.

18
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.490
Always remember you don't want to reform
those edge stitches so they remain purl

19
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.490
stitches as viewed from the private side
that's facing you on the knitting machine.

20
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.660
The trick is to leave two stitches plain
on one side of the knitting

21
00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:59.570
and when the edges are joined,
you'll get the desired results.

22
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.490
Maybe a bit of a brain twister at first.

23
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.180
Right side, wrong side,
private and public sides.

24
00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:12.660
But the bottom line is you want to end up
with two purl stitches on one edge

25
00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:19.610
of a piece and one purl stitch
on the other edge that's going to join.

26
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.800
Take time to plan this ahead
for beautiful 1x1 ribbed seams.

